    "https://phe.tbe.taleo.net/phe02/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=ARKASTAT2&cws=56&rid=39644": {
      "title": "Career Center",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Job Details Please note: all position postings close at 12:00 A.M. CST on the position closing date Assistant Professor 9 Mo Working Title Assistant Professor of Criminology Position # F00220 Department Sociology and Criminology Apply Now Employment Status: Full time (29-40 Hrs) Type of Employment: Faculty # of openings: 1 Location: ASU-Jonesboro Proposed Salary Range: Commensurate with Experience Closing: 10/26/26 Please note: All postings close at 12 A.M. CST on the closing date.  This employer participates in E-Verify. Position Summary: The Department of Sociology and Criminology at Arkansas State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Criminology position beginning in Fall 2027. A Ph.D. in Criminology, Criminal Justice, or a closely related field is required; applicants nearing completion of the doctorate (ABD) may be considered. Area of specialization is open, although the ability to teach courses within the program’s core requirements will be given priority. The ability and willingness to teach in both in-person and online formats is preferred. Duties & Responsibilities: Regular responsibilities include teaching, research, advising and mentoring students, as well as engaging in service at the university, disciplinary, and community levels. The standard teaching load at Arkansas State is 12 credit hours per semester, but there is commonly a semester course release provided for research for pre-tenure faculty. Teaching assignments will include undergraduate courses in criminology and may include graduate-level courses. The successful candidate will teach core courses consistent with program needs while also teaching courses in their areas of expertise and interest. About Arkansas State University and the Department Arkansas State University is a comprehensive R2 public research university located in Jonesboro, Arkansas, approximately 70 miles northwest of Memphis. The department offers a well-rounded curriculum to more than 300 majors in its BA programs in Criminology and Sociology. The department offers a B.A. in Criminology in both traditional and fully online formats, as well as a B.A. in Sociology and an M.A. in Sociology. Knowledge/Skills/Abilities: General Days/Hours: Monday - Friday 8:00 a.m. - 5:00 p.m. Additional hours as requested and/or needed Regular and reliable attendance Other: Minimum Qualifications: An earned Ph.D. in Criminology, Criminal Justice, or a related field is required for this position; applications nearing completion of the doctorate (ABD) may be considered Applications should include the following: a) cover letter describing teaching and research interests b) curriculum vitae c) evidence of teaching effectiveness d) unofficial transcripts e) contact information of three references E-Verify Participation Notice: https://www.e-verify.gov/sites/default/files/everify/posters/EVerifyParticipationPoster.pdf E-Verify Right to Work: https://www.e-verify.gov/sites/default/files/everify/posters/IER_RightToWorkPoster%20Eng_Es.pdf Back Apply Now"
    },
    "https://phe.tbe.taleo.net/phe02/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=ARKASTAT2&cws=56&rid=39667": {
      "title": "Career Center",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Job Details Please note: all position postings close at 12:00 A.M. CST on the position closing date Assistant Professor 9 Mo Working Title Assistant Professor of Health Sciences & Risk Management Position # F00496 Department Health Sciences and Risk Management Apply Now Employment Status: Full time (29-40 Hrs) Type of Employment: Faculty # of openings: 1 Location: ASU-Jonesboro Proposed Salary Range: Commensurate with Experience Closing: 10/31/26 Please note: All postings close at 12 A.M. CST on the closing date.  This employer participates in E-Verify. Position Summary: The Arkansas State University Department of Health Sciences & Risk Management invites applications for an Assistant Professor of Health Sciences & Risk Management (tenure-track) to begin in Fall 2027. The position is full-time (9-month) and tenure-track. Duties & Responsibilities: The primary teaching assignment and research focus will be in the Department’s Disaster Preparedness and Emergency Management Program. The ideal candidate will demonstrate potential for an active, high impact emergency management research program. Responsibilities include producing peer-reviewed publications, presenting at professional conferences, and applying for external funding. Additional responsibilities include preparing, developing, and teaching undergraduate and graduate courses across multiple programs in the Department of Health Sciences and Risk Management. Applicants should have the ability to teach a broad range of courses across disciplines such as emergency management, health and/or public administration, policy, research methods, and program development and evaluation. Faculty are expected to maintain regular office hours, mentor undergraduate and graduate students, and participate in course/program assessment. Faculty are also expected to engage in departmental, college, university, and professional service activities that are in the best interest of the students, university, community, and the discipline at large. The ideal candidate will contribute to the development, assessment, and advancement of academic programs, including curriculum development, program assessment, workforce alignment, and engagement with external stakeholders. Arkansas State University is a public research university located in Jonesboro, AR. Arkansas State University is designated by the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as a “Research 2: High Research Spending and Doctorate Production” institution. The Department of Health Sciences & Risk Management has a multidisciplinary and interdisciplinary focus across its programs in Disaster Preparedness & Emergency Management, Dietetics/Nutritional Science, Public Health, Occupational & Environmental Safety & Health, and Health Studies. Knowledge/Skills/Abilities: • Strong computer/technology skills required, or possess the ability to easily obtain needed skills. (Microsoft Office, Blackboard Learn and/or Canvas, variety of WEB based programs, etc.) • Strong written and verbal communication skills required. General Days/Hours: Monday - Friday 8:00 a.m. - 5:00 p.m. Additional hours as requested and/or needed Regular and reliable attendance Other: Application Instructions Qualified applicants should submit their (1) curriculum vitae, (2) a statement of purpose indicating research interests and teaching philosophy, (3) a writing sample (recent peer-reviewed article or dissertation chapter), and (4) contact information for three academic references, that can speak to your research and teaching, that will be contacted if chosen for an interview. All materials should be submitted online via Taleo, and all items must be submitted to be considered. Application review will begin on the closing date in Taleo and will continue until the position is filled. Questions can be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Amy Hyman, at ahyman@astate.edu. Minimum Qualifications: Minimum qualifications include a Ph.D. or terminal research degree in an emergency management or closely related discipline. Candidates who are ABD will also be considered, provided they obtain their degree by July 1, 2027. Candidates should have a strong research agenda in the emergency management discipline, or a closely related discipline with an emergency management focus, with evidence of peer-reviewed publications and/or external funding, or the potential to quickly develop a strong research agenda and be immediately successful regarding research and in obtaining external funding. Candidates should also have experience with face-to-face and online teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels. E-Verify Participation Notice: https://www.e-verify.gov/sites/default/files/everify/posters/EVerifyParticipationPoster.pdf E-Verify Right to Work: https://www.e-verify.gov/sites/default/files/everify/posters/IER_RightToWorkPoster%20Eng_Es.pdf Back Apply Now"
    },
    "https://phe.tbe.taleo.net/phe02/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=ARKASTAT2&cws=56&rid=39692": {
      "title": "Career Center",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Job Details Please note: all position postings close at 12:00 A.M. CST on the position closing date Project Program Manager Working Title Associate Director of Financial Aid for Technical Operations Position # A02694 Department Financial Aid and Scholarships Apply Now Employment Status: Full time (29-40 Hrs) Type of Employment: Staff # of openings: 1 Location: ASU-Jonesboro Proposed Salary Range: Commensurate with Experience Closing: 9/8/26 Please note: All postings close at 12 A.M. CST on the closing date.  This employer participates in E-Verify. Position Summary: The Associate Director of Financial Aid for Technical Operations provides strategic and operational leadership for the technical infrastructure that supports the awarding, packaging, disbursement, and reporting of federal, state, and institutional financial aid. The incumbent serves as the office’s primary technical authority, partnering closely with the Director of Financial Aid, Information Technology, the Registrar, Student Accounts, and Institutional Research to ensure that aid systems operate accurately, efficiently, and in full compliance with federal and state regulations. This position combines deep functional knowledge of financial aid administration with hands-on technical expertise in the Ellucian Banner Student Information System and structured query language (SQL). Duties & Responsibilities: Evaluate, recommend, and implement technical operational goals focused on enhancing the student financial aid and scholarship experience for students and parents. Lead the technical configuration, maintenance, and continuous improvement of the Ellucian Banner Financial Aid module, including annual cycle preparation, rules tables setup, packaging algorithms, need analysis parameters, fund management, and overall process automation. Collaborate with financial aid senior leadership to outline operational needs and technical requirements for processing institutional, state, and federal student aid. Manage the financial aid components of Course Program of Study (CPOS) compliance, including Banner CPOS module configuration, recalculation of federal aid eligibility based on CPOS-eligible enrollment, and related reporting and aid adjustments; partner with the Registrar’s Office and Information Technology on degree audit integration and data exchange. Design, write, test, and maintain SQL queries and scripts to support operational metrics reporting, data extraction, fund reconciliation, federal and state reporting, audit response, and ad hoc reporting/data needs. Under the direction of the Director of Financial Aid, manage the full lifecycle of electronic data exchange with the U.S. Department of Education, including ISIR (FAFSA) loads, COD reporting for Federal Pell Grant, Federal Teach Grant, Federal Direct Loans, NSLDS submissions, and resolution of file rejects and reconciliation discrepancies. · Assist the HEI Program Coordinator, as needed, with the administration of state grants and scholarships, ensuring accurate enrollment certification, eligibility determination, and timely transmission of state-mandated reports. Coordinate annual Ellucian Banner Financial Aid module roll-over activities, year-end processes, regulatory updates, and implementation of new federal and state aid programs and rule changes. Serve as the technical liaison to Information Technology, vendors, and software partners on system upgrades, patches, integrations, and the evaluation of new technologies. Develop and maintain documentation, standard operating procedures, and internal controls for all technical processes, ensuring continuity of operations and audit readiness. Support the Director of Financial Aid in strategic planning, policy development, and the preparation of materials for federal program reviews, state audits, and institutional Knowledge/Skills/Abilities: Demonstrated experience writing and maintaining SQL queries against a Banner or comparable Oracle-based environment. Demonstrated experience managing technical environments and providing visionary leadership to automate processes and enhance end-user experience. Working knowledge of federal Title IV regulations and applicable state aid program rules. General Days/Hours: Monday - Friday 8:00 a.m. - 500 p.m. Additional hours as requested and/or needed Regular and reliable attendance Other: Preferred Qualifications • Higher Education & Financial Aid – Comprehensive understanding of higher education administration and student services, with specialized expertise in federal Title IV regulations (Pell Grant, Direct Loans, FSEOG, Federal Work-Study) and applicable state grant, scholarship, and tuition assistance programs. • Ellucian Banner - Demonstrated proficiency administering the Ellucian Banner Financial Aid module, including rules-based packaging, fund and budget setup, requirements tracking, regulatory user-defined fields, and integration with Banner Student, Accounts Receivable, and General modules. • SQL & Data Operations - Strong working knowledge of SQL (Oracle PL/SQL preferred) for querying, joining, and manipulating data across Banner tables; ability to build reproducible reports including Argos, troubleshoot data integrity issues, and translate functional requirements into accurate technical specifications. • Compliance & Analysis - Skill in interpreting and applying complex federal and state regulations; sound analytical judgment; meticulous attention to detail; and the ability to maintain confidentiality of student records in accordance with FERPA and institutional policy. • Communication & Leadership - Effective written and verbal communication with both technical and non-technical audiences; ability to lead cross-functional projects, mentor staff, and build collaborative relationships across the institution- Minimum Qualifications: An earned Bachelor's Degree Other job-related education and/or experience may be substituted for all or part of these basic requirements upon approval of the qualifications review committee E-Verify Participation Notice: https://www.e-verify.gov/sites/default/files/everify/posters/EVerifyParticipationPoster.pdf E-Verify Right to Work: https://www.e-verify.gov/sites/default/files/everify/posters/IER_RightToWorkPoster%20Eng_Es.pdf Back Apply Now"
    },
    "https://phe.tbe.taleo.net/phe02/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=ARKASTAT2&cws=56&rid=39690": {
      "title": "Career Center",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Job Details Please note: all position postings close at 12:00 A.M. CST on the position closing date Project Program Manager Working Title Associate Director of Scholarships Position # A00885 Department Financial Aid and Scholarships Apply Now Employment Status: Full time (29-40 Hrs) Type of Employment: Staff # of openings: 1 Location: ASU-Jonesboro Proposed Salary Range: Commensurate with Experience Closing: 9/8/26 Please note: All postings close at 12 A.M. CST on the closing date.  This employer participates in E-Verify. Position Summary: The Associate Director of Scholarships is responsible for the overall management of the scholarship programs. This position is responsible for the daily operations of the scholarships unit through creation of policies and procedures, oversight of full-time employees, and monitoring and evaluating the effectiveness and productivity of the scholarship program. The Associate Director of Scholarships provides leadership and sets goals for the Scholarships unit under the general direction of the Director of Financial Aid & Scholarships. This position collaborates across campus departments for effective and accurate service to students and families. Duties & Responsibilities: Evaluate and track current scholarship programs for optimum recruiting and retention effectiveness. Ensures financial sustainability of scholarship programs through strategic planning and optimization of available funding Oversee the processing, balancing, and reconciliation of scholarships programs, including, institutional, privately funded, pass-through, and departmental scholarships Provide assistance and resources to potential and current students, parents, faculty, staff, off-campus agencies, high school counselors, and scholarship donors Prepare and present institutional reports Review the processing of scholarship awards determinations, ensuring awards meet institutional and donor scholarship criteria Oversee work by staff and third-party vendors to ensure scholarship administration is effective and appropriate Work with staff to resolve complex issues involving eligibility, funding, awarding, and disbursement of funds Provide continuing education to scholarship unit staff and general financial aid staff for all regulatory, institutional, and state changes Work closely with Enrollment Management Division (Admissions and Registrar) to align scholarship activities necessary for recruitment and retention of students Develop, implement, maintain, and revise policies and procedures to ensure compliance with all laws, rules, and regulations Supervise scholarships staff and provide support to general financial aid staff regarding scholarship operations Perform as other duties as assigned Knowledge/Skills/Abilities: Ability to supervise employees Ability to interpret laws, guidelines and regulations pertaining to financial aid and scholarship assistance Ability to review and evaluate student needs Ability to manage financial accounting for disbursement and reconciliation of scholarship accounts Knowledge of student financial aid programs, standards, and procedures Knowledge of counseling and interview techniques Excellent communication skills Experience with Microsoft Office Products Familiarity with Ellucian Banner products Experience in web-based technology and demonstrated use of technology in delivery of financial aid services General Days/Hours: Monday - Friday 8:00 a.m. - 5:00 p.m. Additional hours as requested and/or needed Regular and reliable attendance Other: Please note: all position postings close at 12:00 A.M. CST on the position closing date Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree preferred with emphasis in Educational Administration, College Student Personnel Services, or a related field Five (5) years of experience in higher education financial aid office Minimum Qualifications: An earned Bachelor's Degree E-Verify Participation Notice: https://www.e-verify.gov/sites/default/files/everify/posters/EVerifyParticipationPoster.pdf E-Verify Right to Work: https://www.e-verify.gov/sites/default/files/everify/posters/IER_RightToWorkPoster%20Eng_Es.pdf Back Apply Now"
    },
    "https://phe.tbe.taleo.net/phe02/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=ARKASTAT2&cws=56&rid=38683": {
      "title": "Career Center",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Job Details Please note: all position postings close at 12:00 A.M. CST on the position closing date Laboratory Technician Working Title Clinical Skills Laboratory Technician (Animal Handling & Student Teaching Focused) Position # A02686 Department College of Veterinary Medicine Apply Now Employment Status: Full time (29-40 Hrs) Type of Employment: Staff # of openings: 1 Location: ASU-Jonesboro Proposed Salary Range: Commensurate with Experience Closing: 9/15/26 Please note: All postings close at 12 A.M. CST on the closing date.  This employer participates in E-Verify. Position Summary: The College of Veterinary Medicine is recruiting a Registered Veterinary Technician to support the teaching mission within the Veterinary Clinical Skills Program. The primary purpose of this position is to promote and support education of veterinary students in clinical simulation laboratories. This position performs a wide variety of instructional and managerial duties for the Clinical Skills Program. Duties & Responsibilities: Instruct & Supervise: Assist Clinical Skills faculty in instructing and supervising veterinary students during laboratory sessions in the pre-clinical curriculum (Years 1–3). Lab Preparation & Execution: Prepare, set up, and conduct clinical skills laboratories, ensuring all materials, models, and specimens are ready for instruction. Demonstration & Coaching: Demonstrate clinical procedures and techniques using simulations and live animals, providing skill-based coaching and constructive feedback to    students. Assessment Support: Set up and assist in administering Objective Structured Clinical Examinations (OSCEs). Lab & Equipment Stewardship: Maintain all teaching laboratories, equipment, and storage areas to a high standard of organization, cleanliness, and operational readiness. Curriculum Delivery: Utilize diverse teaching tools—including simulation models, anatomic specimens, and live animals—to train students in clinical skills across principal  veterinary species (e.g., dogs, cats, horses, livestock). Asset Management: Oversee the full lifecycle of teaching assets, including the inventory, maintenance, repair, and sterilization of simulation models and surgical equipment. Supply Chain Coordination: Facilitate the procurement, receipt, and organization of all laboratory equipment, supplies, and consumables. Collaboration: Cultivate effective, collaborative working relationships with faculty, staff, students, and external stakeholders in support of the A-State CVM mission. Other Duties: Perform other related duties as assigned. Knowledge/Skills/Abilities: Animal Handling & Safety: Proficiency in handling, restraining, and working safely with dogs, cats, horses, cattle, and small ruminants. Knowledge of standard veterinary safety and restraint protocols. Ability to obtain certification from the Arkansas Board of Animal Health. Clinical & Laboratory Practice: Working knowledge of aseptic technique and clinical procedures. Assist with oversight of instructional models, including storage, maintenance, setup, and takedown, ensuring they are fully functional for lab sessions. Physical & Operational Capacity: Ability to push, pull, and lift items weighing up to 70 lb (32 kg). Possess a valid driver's license and ability to operate a vehicle within the state of Arkansas. Administrative Competence: Foundational knowledge of basic accounting principles. Ability to handle confidential information with discretion. Proficiency in Microsoft 365 and Office Suite applications. Professional Skills: Excellent written and oral communication skills. Strong critical thinking and organizational abilities. Capacity to multitask effectively in a dynamic academic team centered environment. General Days/Hours: Monday - Friday 8:00 a.m. - 5:00 p.m. Starting and ending hours may change seasonally Regular and reliable attendance Other: Please note: all position postings close at 12:00 A.M. CST on the position closing date Minimum Qualifications: Registered/Licensed veterinary technician (RVT) Other job-related education and/or experience may be substituted for all or part of these basic requirements, except for certification or licensure requirements, upon approval of the qualifications review committee Preferred Qualification: RVT plus at least two years’ experience E-Verify Participation Notice: https://www.e-verify.gov/sites/default/files/everify/posters/EVerifyParticipationPoster.pdf E-Verify Right to Work: https://www.e-verify.gov/sites/default/files/everify/posters/IER_RightToWorkPoster%20Eng_Es.pdf Back Apply Now"
    },
    "https://phe.tbe.taleo.net/phe02/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=ARKASTAT2&cws=56&rid=38684": {
      "title": "Career Center",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Job Details Please note: all position postings close at 12:00 A.M. CST on the position closing date Laboratory Technician Working Title Clinical Skills Laboratory Technician (Model-Focused) Position # A02685 Department College of Veterinary Medicine Apply Now Employment Status: Full time (29-40 Hrs) Type of Employment: Staff # of openings: 1 Location: ASU-Jonesboro Proposed Salary Range: Commensurate with Experience Closing: 9/15/26 Please note: All postings close at 12 A.M. CST on the closing date.  This employer participates in E-Verify. Position Summary: The College of Veterinary Medicine is seeking a creative and hands-on technician to join our Clinical Skills team. The ideal candidate is a problem-solver at heart—someone who thrives on designing, building, and refining practical solutions. This role merges technical craftsmanship with educational support, requiring a unique blend of innovation and physical skill. The primary purpose of this position is to promote and support education of veterinary students in the clinical simulation laboratories. This position performs a wide variety of instructional and managerial duties for the Clinical Skills Program. Duties & Responsibilities: Assist Clinical Skills faculty in the instruction and supervision of veterinary students during laboratory sessions in the pre-clinical curriculum (Years 1–3). Prepare, set up, and conduct clinical skills laboratories, ensuring all materials, models, and specimens are ready for instruction. Demonstrate clinical procedures and techniques to students and provide constructive, skill-based feedback during practice sessions. Support the scheduling and logistical coordination of laboratory training sessions, practical examinations, and related academic activities. Set up and help to administer objective structured clinical skills examinations (OSCEs). Maintain teaching laboratories, equipment, and storage areas to a high standard of organization, cleanliness, and operational readiness. Utilize a variety of teaching tools—including simulation models, anatomic specimens (cadavers), and live animals—to train students in clinical skills across principal veterinary    species (e.g., dogs, cats, horses, livestock). Develop, prototype, and fabricate novel teaching models and simulators; maintain and troubleshoot 3-D printing equipment and software. Oversee the inventory, maintenance, repair, and sterilization of simulation models and related teaching assets. Facilitate the procurement, receipt, and organization of laboratory equipment, supplies, and consumables. Cultivate and maintain effective, collaborative working relationships with faculty, staff, students, and external stakeholders in support of the A-State CVM mission. Perform other related duties as assigned. Knowledge/Skills/Abilities: Experience in designing, prototyping, or fabricating functional simulation models, trainers, or related physical prototypes. Hands-on aptitude with a range of fabrication techniques (e.g., 3D printing, molding/casting, basic machining, or electronics). Ability to safely lift, push, and pull items up to 70 lb (32 kg). Valid driver’s license and ability to operate a vehicle within Arkansas. Proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite or similar productivity software. Excellent organizational, communication, and critical-thinking skills. Capacity to work effectively in a dynamic, collaborative academic environment. General Days/Hours: Monday - Friday 8:00 a.m. - 5:00 p.m. • Starting and ending hours may change seasonally Regular and reliable attendance Other: Please note: all position postings close at 12:00 A.M. CST on the position closing date Minimum Qualifications: An earned high school diploma or equivalent. Some prior experience in an academic, clinical, or veterinary setting regarding additive manufacturing (3D printing/scanning), working with composites/resins, or welding. Two years experience handling large or small animals Preferred Qualification: Demonstrated portfolio or examples of created medical or veterinary simulation models. Formal training or significant experience in additive manufacturing (3D printing/scanning), working with composites/resins, or welding. Foundational knowledge of basic accounting or supply inventory management. E-Verify Participation Notice: https://www.e-verify.gov/sites/default/files/everify/posters/EVerifyParticipationPoster.pdf E-Verify Right to Work: https://www.e-verify.gov/sites/default/files/everify/posters/IER_RightToWorkPoster%20Eng_Es.pdf Back Apply Now"
    },
    "https://phe.tbe.taleo.net/phe02/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=ARKASTAT2&cws=56&rid=39113": {
      "title": "Career Center",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Job Details Please note: all position postings close at 12:00 A.M. CST on the position closing date Administrative Analyst Working Title Course Inventory Specialist Position # A00954 Department Records and Registration Apply Now Employment Status: Full time (29-40 Hrs) Type of Employment: Staff # of openings: 1 Location: ASU-Jonesboro Proposed Salary Range: $35,354.00 Closing: 9/11/26 Please note: All postings close at 12 A.M. CST on the closing date.  This employer participates in E-Verify. Position Summary: The Administrative Analyst is responsible for conducting special research studies, analyzing data, preparing statistics, making recommendations based on research findings, and monitoring and coordinating project/program activities. This position is governed by state and federal laws and agency/institution policy. Duties & Responsibilities: Coordinates development of the University bulletin and course catalog and associated materials (e.g., maintaining course inventory in the student information system, updating website accordingly, researching new methodologies for implementation to improve usability and functionality of the bulletin) Reviews curriculum proposals providing advisory recommendations. Assists in maintenance of degree evaluation systems, check sheets, degree plans, and additional methods used to assist students with the graduation process. Maintains Registrar’s Office section of the University’s website, working with academic areas to ensure information is accurate, up-to-date, and user-friendly Develops and maintains procedures and manuals for various student information system functions Assists with training departments on student information systems and Registrar’s Office functions Provides expertise related to course requirements for assisting departments in registering students. Other duties as assigned Knowledge/Skills/Abilities: Knowledge of planning, research, and analysis techniques and procedures, department operations, policies, and procedures, applicable laws and regulations, and basic accounting principles Ability to plan and execute systems and organizational analysis and feasibility studies, conduct research and perform quantitative quality assurance reviews, research, prepare, and present comprehensive written and oral reports, and organize and conduct meetings and workshops General Days/Hours: Monday - Friday 8:00 a.m. - 5:00 p.m. Additional hours as requested and/or needed Regular and reliable attendance Other: Please note: all position postings close at 12:00 A.M. CST on the position closing date Experience with Adobe Creative Suite, particularly Adobe inDesign, and Microsoft Office Suite or comparable software. Experience with social media platform. Copyediting experience preferred. Basic knowledge of content management systems and HTML. Close attention to detail needed. Minimum Qualifications: The formal education equivalent of an earned Bachelor's Degree in public administration, general business, or a related field Other job related education and/or experience may be substituted for all or part of these basic requirements, except for certification or licensure requirements, upon approval of the qualifications review committee E-Verify Participation Notice: https://www.e-verify.gov/sites/default/files/everify/posters/EVerifyParticipationPoster.pdf E-Verify Right to Work: https://www.e-verify.gov/sites/default/files/everify/posters/IER_RightToWorkPoster%20Eng_Es.pdf Back Apply Now"
